revit has come under fire in the press  lately and today we're going to talk  about  revit versus architects  what's going on guys my name is david  thomage and welcome back to the channel  today we're talking about the open  letter addressing the concerns of  revit users predominantly in the uk  if you aren't aware already 17 out of 25  firms publicly put their name forward  to the ceo of autodesk which is the  parent company of revit  to say that there are many problems that  they need to address  publicly more so than privately because  clearly  revit and autodesk are not listening to  these concerns during private  conversations  some of these firms are the world's  absolute best architectural firms  including grimshaw and zaha hadid  architects  if you don't know who these two firms  are quickly google them and you'll be  shocked at what they've done  they produce some of the absolute best  masterpieces that we have in our  generation between the 25 firms that  allowed their names to be on the list  and didn't allow their name to be  publicly addressed these firms hold  a total of 5 000 licenses or an  equivalent  of 22 million dollars worth of annual  revenue for autodesk so what's the  problem what are these firms  really looking for well i've read the  letter in great detail i've read all the  articles that go with it  and i've also read the responses from  autodesk basically  what these firms really want is revit to  improve  they're not looking for anything else  but  the improvement of the actual platform  itself most of these companies feel like  the architectural section of revit has  been pushed to the side for the past  couple years  whilst autodesk has focused on the  engineering side of it  another factor that many of them are  concerned about is the continual  growth of price per year over the last  five years there have been  five different pricing platforms that  companies have had to adopt to  revvit has gone from a very basic  pricing model  to a very complicated pay-per-year  annual recurring fee  some of these customers are even saying  that their costs  for revit alone has gone up 70 percent  now i don't know about you guys but if  somebody put up the price by  70 over a number years i would be pretty  upset as well  businesses have to run on very tight  margins  and architectural programs not just  revit but everything that goes along  with the suite of running an  architectural  firm is a huge chunk of your overheads  the main reason they're concerned about  the price increase isn't actually to do  with the fact that it's getting more  expensive  it's more so to do with the fact that  revit is no longer innovating  autodesk is no longer innovating they  have stopped  putting all their eggs into the  architectural basket and started taking  them all out into the engineering side  of things  it has been a very long time since  architecture  has had the main focus of the update  there's been no  innovations to save time to further  innovate  it's just been stale and stagnant for a  couple years  whilst the costs have continued to go up  so these architectural firms have not  been able  to justify the increasing price without  having a major update  that comes along with it it would be  absolutely fine  if every year something major change in  revit to either save you time  save you money or save something along  the way  instead it's just making things more and  more complicated  as designs get more and more complex  these firms are also finding that the  salespeople of autodesk are pushing  these products on them  now as a cause of this there's been a  lack of trust  the users of revit no longer trust the  system  and trust the manufacturer being  autodesk they're very worried now about  what's going to happen  as autodesk continues to transition to  the cloud  how secure is their data going to be who  is able to access this data  what is that data going to be used for  besides from just inside their actual  firm  now as we know privacy and data is a  very very hot topic  in 2020 and has been for the past decade  the more the data gets leaked the more  that information gets out that shouldn't  be out there  the more we crack down on privacy and  data settings  now this is a very fair concern from  these companies  imagine if you had just spent hundreds  of millions of dollars  setting up a massive architectural firm  setting up  all of your templates all of your  libraries  absolutely everything and somebody was  able to access one of your files  those hundreds of millions of dollars  you just spent  somebody stole for free basically offer  you  that isn't a very good feeling and that  is a very real concern  for these big firms out there now these  firms are looking  to rebuild the trust to rebuild the  relationship with autodesk  because obviously they have spent  hundreds of millions of dollars  training their staff building their  platforms building their templates  they're not going to jump ship no matter  what the price of revit  actually goes up to they really want to  be open and transparent with this  company  to make sure it continues to progress  and excel  because if it doesn't then the companies  are going to get stale  stagnant and they're not going to be  able to be these award-winning firms  that they are today  they basically asked autodesk to provide  a roadmap of what the future looks like  what they can expect and how they are  going to innovate  more and more each year now autodesk was  very quick to respond  to this open letter obviously they had  to protect their assets and they had to  protect their company  so they came out firing they were not  happy with some of the comments  but they also agreed with some of the  points made  ever since the first release they've  released an additional two statements  totaling three statements at this point  in time  addressing the concerns that these  companies have put together  one of the main points that i actually  took from the these responses is the  fact  that autodesk or revit has actually  openly admitted  that they have focused a lot of their  time energy  and money into the engineering and  construction side of things forever  they've put the architectural systems on  the back burner  because they felt that it was very well  developed and this other area  needed improvement which yes is  definitely true  but when most of your customer base is  architectural you still need  to reward those guys for staying with  you and building  such a great platform and such a great  software what they didn't say though  is that they will actually put some  active effort back into the  architectural industry  they've just said yes we're focused on  engineering  and construction because that's where  the future is so i don't know how  effective this letter is going to be at  pushing those updates quicker into the  software i have a feeling that revit is  going to continue to focus on  engineering and construction  because that's where the future they see  is the ceo directly responded saying  that they will talk  personally and privately with the 17  companies that made their name public  which means the 17 companies are going  to score some sort of deal behind the  scenes cut their costs  and try and get in on a ground floor to  these new innovative products  what it also means is only those 17  companies  are going to have those discussions with  the ceo and with autodesk  the rest of the users that use revit  well they're going to see the effects  10 20 years down the track now maybe  reading into the comments of the  cloud-based  response a little bit too much but  basically what the ceo stated in direct  response to the concerns  of the autodesk users was the simple  fact that  revit and autodesk are meeting the  minimum standards  for cloud-based services the minimum  standards  doesn't mean you're actively trying to  defend  people's privacy it means you're just  kind of  scraping by to be able to produce that  product and be  in that space i'm hoping to see the  cloud-based software  really take a new leg forward and really  leap  because cloud-based software is  definitely the future  and if privacy becomes a concern it can  [ __ ] a company  it could be a matter of days before  revit goes from  the absolute pinnacle and best software  in the industry  to a company that's absolutely bankrupt  because somebody has leaked all the  information from their cloud  that would be a huge blow to the  industry as a whole  autodesk also published a roadmap of  what the future looks like  for revit in 2021 and 2022  now this is something that they do every  year so it wasn't  really like they went out of their way  to showcase all their new features that  are in development and they're thinking  about producing and making  they're just being open and transparent  as they have always been  in the past i don't think that's what  the  signatories of the open letter really  wanted  i think that they wanted more  reassurance  to know that the future is going to be  focused on architecture  in the open road map unfortunately when  you scroll through it  you will see that yes architecture is  getting a little bit of an upgrade  but it is again very very heavily  focused on  engineering and construction a point i  did find in the roadmap was revit was  really pushing to move away  from a desktop only program they're  trying to make the future  more mobile that means cloud-based  systems that can run on mobiles  tablets basically anywhere this would be  a very very good innovative step for  architecture  architects engineers absolutely  everybody that uses the software  it was pretty well hidden in that road  map  but they're definitely talking about  moving away from the desktop only system  and being able to document design on any  platform  overall what are my thoughts well i  think these companies have a right to be  upset  they're paying a premium for one of the  best software's in the world  unfortunately they're not getting the  best software and the best  customer service in return i would be  upset  if i was paying hundreds of thousands of  dollars per year to a company  and not getting my money's worth however  at the same time  i see it from a business point of view  as well autodesk is a giant company  they're a world leader in the industry  and they want to dominate and continue  dominating  which means from a business point of  view and a marketing point of view  sometimes you have to shelf one idea to  make the other one  grow and in this case that is exactly  what happened  they shelved architecture to make sure  engineering and construction  became just as powerful and made the  software even better than what it is  today  anyway that's all for me today guys  thank you so much for watching i hope  you enjoyed the video  if you did make sure you smash that like  button to help this video with the  algorithm  and if you want to see more of my  content make sure you smash that  subscribe button 2020 style  and as always i'll see you next monday

